  <title>7.17.5: Equations of Graph Translations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02041</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.5: Equations of Graph Translations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6bc9d233-6900-4fd3-8e1a-8be93a7b6707</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is a graph that represents \(y=x^2\). </item>
</list>
<image height="230" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/95133170df377117169b0f4e25c3315ccced783f" width="254"/><para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. On the same coordinate plane, sketch a graph that represents \(y=(x−6)^2+10\). </para>
<para>Select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<image height="312" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/65c1dd64b7648e12977967f98a128f35306943a2" width="312"/><para>A graph the same shape as \(y=x^2\) but shifted 6 units to the right and 10 units upward. Its vertex is at \((6,10)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph representing \(y=x^2\) is shifted 2 units to the left, 10 units down, and flipped so that it opens downward, as shown. </item>
</list>
<image height="230" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f92b9aa8f9d9d15e3e8bc3fda5ab1cec79e365b1" width="254"/><para>Which equation defines this curve?</para>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-(x+2)^2−10\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x+2)^2−10\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-(x+2)^2+10\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x+2)^2+10\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-(x+2)^2−10\)</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will revisit vertex form at the end of Unit 9. This cool down goes beyond the understanding of vertex form students need to demonstrate on the Unit 7 Quiz.</para></section>
</content>
